---
import BlogArticleShell from '../../../components/BlogArticleShell.astro';
import BlogArticleInlineFigures from '../../../components/BlogArticleInlineFigures.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const canonicalPath = '/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-browse-navigation-guide';
const publishedISO = '2026-06-07';
---

<BlogArticleShell
  title="How to browse the Kakobuy Spreadsheet: homepage grid, categories & full directory | kakobuy-cn.com"
  description="A plain-language map of how kakobuy-cn.com is organised—featured picks, twelve category lanes, deep catalogue pools, and the external Spreadsheet tutorial—so you find links without treating every page the same."
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  headline="Browse navigation guide: homepage, categories, and the full Kakobuy Spreadsheet directory"
  publishedISO={publishedISO}
  displayDate="7 June 2026"
  structuredKeywords={[
    'Kakobuy Spreadsheet browse',
    'kakobuy-cn.com categories',
    'spreadsheet directory navigation',
    'how to find Kakobuy links',
    'featured grid vs category pages',
  ]}
>
  <p class="blog-lede" slot="lede">
    New visitors often land on our homepage, see a product grid, and ask a fair question: <em>“Is this the whole spreadsheet, or is there more?”</em>
    Both—and neither.
    <strong><a href="/">kakobuy-cn.com</a></strong> is a moderated <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> directory with <strong>three browse layers</strong> (featured picks, category lanes, and deep pools) plus an external tutorial hub for power users.
    This guide explains what each layer is for, when to use it, and how paste hygiene stays the same no matter where you clicked.
  </p>

  <h2>The three layers (mental model)</h2>
    <BlogArticleInlineFigures slug="kakobuy-spreadsheet-browse-navigation-guide" />

  <div class="blog-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Layer</th>
          <th scope="col">Where it lives</th>
          <th scope="col">Best for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Featured grid</strong></td>
          <td>Homepage upper section</td>
          <td>Fast orientation—“what’s active this week”</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Category lanes</strong></td>
          <td><a href="/category/shoes/">Shoes</a>, <a href="/category/hoodies/">Hoodies</a>, <a href="/category/perfume/">Perfume</a>, etc.</td>
          <td>Shopping by wardrobe goal with extra lane context</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Deep pools</strong></td>
          <td>Behind the UI (40+ marketplace lanes)</td>
          <td>Power browsing via external spreadsheet bridges</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Think of the homepage as a <em>newsstand</em>, category pages as <em>aisles</em>, and the <a href="/spreadsheet/">Spreadsheet tutorial hub</a> as the <em>warehouse map</em> veterans keep open in another tab.
  </p>

  <h2>Layer 1 — Featured grid (“daily picks”)</h2>
  <p>
    The grid near the top of the homepage is intentionally small.
    Curators rotate high-signal rows so repeat visitors see movement without scrolling a thousand cards on mobile.
    Sellers in China rename shops, swap batches, and kill albums overnight—so featured picks emphasize <strong>fresh links</strong>, not completeness.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a featured card’s link fails, that is normal link rot—not a site outage.
    Jump to the matching <strong>category lane</strong> or the full directory flow described below; our <a href="/blog/spreadsheet-row-archiving-vanished-listings/">vanished listings guide</a> explains why rows disappear.
  </p>

  <h2>Layer 2 — Twelve homepage category shortcuts</h2>
  <p>
    Below the hero you will find twelve pills that map to shopper language, not factory taxonomy:
    <strong>Shoes, T-Shirts, Pants, Jackets, Hoodies, Bags, Accessories, Electronics, Headwear, Jersey, Perfume, Other</strong>.
    Each shortcut aggregates deeper marketplace lanes—for example, <a href="/category/shoes/">Shoes</a> pulls from sneaker pools plus boots and slides.
  </p>
  <p>
    Category pages add <strong>unique editorial context</strong> (sizing notes, freight cautions, lane-specific FAQs) that the homepage grid does not repeat.
    They are the best starting point when you know your wardrobe goal:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Footwear → <a href="/blog/kakobuy-sneakers-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">sneakers lane guide</a></li>
    <li>Fragrance → <a href="/blog/kakobuy-perfume-fragrance-spreadsheet-lane-2026/">perfume lane guide</a></li>
    <li>Outerwear → <a href="/blog/kakobuy-jackets-outerwear-spreadsheet-lane-2026/">jackets &amp; parkas guide</a></li>
    <li>Brand curiosity → <a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-brands-catalog-2026/">2026 brand catalogue</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Layer 3 — Deep pools &amp; the external spreadsheet hub</h2>
  <p>
    Behind the friendly twelve-lane UI sits a wider catalogue—roughly <strong>1,000+ rows</strong> and <strong>140+ brand labels</strong> in our June&nbsp;2026 export—organised into <strong>40+ deep lanes</strong> (sneakers, parkas, jewellery, tracksuits, etc.).
    The on-site category cards show a diversified slice per lane; the highest-volume grid many veterans use lives on external spreadsheet bridges linked from our <a href="/spreadsheet/">Spreadsheet tutorial</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the tutorial hub when:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You already understand paste workflow (<a href="/how-to-buy/">How to buy</a>).</li>
    <li>You want spreadsheet-style scanning, filters, or curator columns.</li>
    <li>You are cross-checking community hubs—read <a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-hubs-compared/">hubs compared</a> for safety context.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What product detail pages are (and are not)</h2>
  <p>
    Clicking a card opens a product detail page with rich photos and paste-ready context.
    Those pages exist to help you evaluate a row before copying the marketplace URL into Kakobuy.
    They are <strong>not</strong> a checkout surface—commerce still happens on <a href="https://kakobuy.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kakobuy</a> after paste.
    Treat cards as <em>orientation</em>; treat Kakobuy inbound QC as <em>evidence</em> (<a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-qc/">QC guide</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>One paste workflow—every entry point</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Pick a layer</strong> (featured, category, or external sheet) based on how much scrolling you tolerate.</li>
    <li><strong>Open the live listing</strong> and copy the canonical URL—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-links-discovery-url-hygiene/">URL hygiene</a> matters more than which page you started from.</li>
    <li><strong>Paste into Kakobuy</strong>; freeze size/colour variants before domestic pay (<a href="/blog/kakobuy-taobao/">Taobao</a>, <a href="/blog/kakobuy-weidian/">Weidian</a>, <a href="/blog/kakobuy-1688/">1688</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Approve warehouse photos</strong>, then consolidate and ship (<a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-to-parcel-checklist/">Spreadsheet → parcel checklist</a>).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common navigation mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Treating featured grid as exhaustive:</strong> it is curated, not complete—use categories next.</li>
    <li><strong>Searching only brand names on-site:</strong> our taxonomy mirrors seller strings; missing brand ≠ impossible factory.</li>
    <li><strong>Skipping category context:</strong> lane guides exist because freight and QC differ—tees and perfume do not ship alike.</li>
    <li><strong>Replacing Kakobuy with DMs:</strong> WhatsApp “private agents” trade safety for hype—<a href="/blog/web-spreadsheets-vs-whatsapp-sellers-safety/">2026 safety report</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where the homepage intro fits</h2>
  <p>
    Scroll to the bottom of the homepage and you will find a short “New to Kakobuy?” section in plain language—what we curate, why links rotate, and how Kakobuy handles QC and freight.
    Read it once, then bookmark the <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet hub</a></strong> category that matches your haul.
    That is the whole navigation secret: <em>picks for speed, categories for clarity, spreadsheet hub for depth—and Kakobuy for truth.</em>
  </p>

  <h2>Pillar companions &amp; lane guides</h2>
  <p>
    Navigation is easier with two bookmarked essays: this guide (how layers fit) and the <a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-brands-catalog-2026/">2026 brand catalogue</a> (what labels appear in metadata).
    From there, open lane-specific guides instead of scrolling blindly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/blog/kakobuy-sneakers-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">Sneakers lane</a> · <a href="/blog/kakobuy-hoodies-sweatshirts-spreadsheet-lane-2026/">Hoodies lane</a> · <a href="/blog/kakobuy-tracksuits-pants-spreadsheet-lane-2026/">Pants &amp; tracksuits</a></li>
    <li><a href="/blog/kakobuy-bags-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">Bags lane</a> · <a href="/blog/kakobuy-accessories-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">Accessories lane</a> · <a href="/blog/kakobuy-jewelry-watches-spreadsheet-lane-2026/">Jewelry &amp; watches</a></li>
    <li><a href="/blog/kakobuy-first-haul-budget-planner-2026/">First haul budget</a> · <a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-to-parcel-checklist/">Spreadsheet → parcel checklist</a></li>
  </ul>
</BlogArticleShell>
